        {
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "bold"
            }
          ],
          "text": "confusing assignment (=) with comparison (==)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". Remember, a single equals sign (`=`) "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "italic"
            }
          ],
          "text": "assigns"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " a value to a variable (e.g., `score = 100`), while the double equals sign (`==`) "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "marks": [
            {
              "type": "italic"
            }
          ],
          "text": "checks"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " if two values are equal (e.g., `if score == 100:`). Mixing these up can lead to logic errors that are hard to spot in your "
